@article{lourie_scruples_2021,
	title = {{SCRUPLES}: {A} {Corpus} of {Community} {Ethical} {Judgments} on 32,000 {Real}-{Life} {Anecdotes}},
	volume = {35},
	issn = {2374-3468, 2159-5399},
	shorttitle = {{SCRUPLES}},
	url = {https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/17589},
	doi = {10.1609/aaai.v35i15.17589},
	abstract = {As AI systems become an increasing part of people's everyday lives, it becomes ever more important that they understand people's ethical norms. Motivated by descriptive ethics, a field of study that focuses on people's descriptive judgments rather than theoretical prescriptions on morality, we investigate a novel, data-driven approach to machine ethics.

We introduce SCRUPLES, the first large-scale dataset with 625,000 ethical judgments over 32,000 real-life anecdotes. Each anecdote recounts a complex ethical situation, often posing moral dilemmas, paired with a distribution of judgments contributed by the community members. Our dataset presents a major challenge to state-of-the-art neural language models, leaving significant room for improvement. However, when presented with simplified moral situations, the results are considerably more promising, suggesting that neural models can effectively learn simpler ethical building blocks.

A key take-away of our empirical analysis is that norms are not always clean-cut; many situations are naturally divisive. We present a new method to estimate the best possible performance on such tasks with inherently diverse label distributions, and explore likelihood functions that separate intrinsic from model uncertainty. Data and code are available at https://github.com/allenai/scruples.},
	number = {15},
	urldate = {2025-08-15},
	journal = {Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence},
	author = {Lourie, Nicholas and Le Bras, Ronan and Choi, Yejin},
	month = may,
	year = {2021},
	note = {TLDR: This work introduces SCRUPLES, the first large-scale dataset with 625,000 ethical judgments over 32,000 real-life anecdotes, and presents a new method to estimate the best possible performance on such tasks with inherently diverse label distributions, and explores likelihood functions that separate intrinsic from model uncertainty.},
	keywords = {Text Classification \& Sentiment Analysis},
	pages = {13470--13479},
	annote = {SCRUPLES
根据您提供的论文《SCRUPLES: A Corpus of Community Ethical Judgments on 32,000 Real-life Anecdotes》，以下是从数据集、指标、任务和结论四个维度的总结，以及对实验和数据格式的详细描述：
1. 论文总结


数据集 (Dataset):


SCRUPLES：包含约 32,000 条真实生活轶事（Anecdotes）和 625,000 条社区道德评判。数据源自 Reddit 论坛，反映了现实中人们对道德问题的多样化观点。


DILEMMAS ：作为补充，包含 10,000 对简单动作的道德排序（如：相比于“在图书馆大声说话”，“在室友食物里放欧芹”哪个更不道德）。




评测指标 (Metric):


除了传统的 准确率 (Accuracy) 和 F1 分数，论文重点采用了 交叉熵 (Cross-Entropy)。


BEST 指标 ：提出了一种全新的贝叶斯估计方法（Bayesian Estimated Score Terminus），用于在存在“道德歧义”和标注不一致的情况下，估算模型能达到的理论最高表现上限。




任务 (Task):


“谁错了？” (WHO'S IN THE WRONG?)：给定一段轶事文字，模型需要预测社区成员对该行为的评判分布。这不仅仅是分类，更是对群体道德倾向的分布预测 。




结论 (Conclusion):


道德规范并非非黑即白：许多现实场景具有天然的争议性。


叙事理解是核心挑战：模型在处理简单的动作排序（DILEMMAS）时表现较好，但在理解复杂的轶事文本时面临极大困难 。


概率建模更有效：使用能够捕捉不确定性的模型层（如 Dirichlet-multinomial）比简单的分类器表现更优 。





2. 实验是怎么做的？（流程描述）
实验旨在测试人工智能是否能像人类一样理解复杂的道德规范 ：


数据抓取与清理 ：从 Reddit 的特定板块抓取原始贴文。通过过滤规则剔除无效、太短或被删除的评论。


属性提取 ：利用正则表达式从贴文中提取标题、正文、事件类型（已发生的 HISTORICAL 或 假设的 HYPOTHETICAL）以及具体的行为描述（Action）。


计算金标准 (Gold Labels) ：统计每条贴文下社区成员的投票（如“作者错”、“对方错”、“都没错”等），将其标准化为概率分布作为模型的预测目标。


模型训练 ：对比了 BERT、RoBERTa 等预训练模型。创新点在于修改了模型的最后一层，将传统的 Softmax 替换为 Dirichlet-Multinomial 层，以便分离模型自身的不确定性和问题本质的争议性 。



3. 数据集格式与样例
数据集格式 (Dataset Format)
每个数据实例包含以下核心字段 ：


Title (标题)：简述故事核心。


Text (正文)：详细的叙事背景。


Action (行为)：从标题中提取的动名词短语，概括作者做了什么 。


Scores (得分分布)：记录了社区对五个类别的投票数（AUTHOR, OTHER, EVERYBODY, NOBODY, INFO）。


Type (类型)：标记是真实发生的历史事件还是假设场景 。


样例展示 (Example)

标题 (Title): 在推销员面前关门？正文 (Text): 刚才有个保险公司的推销员敲我家门。我还没等他开口就说“抱歉，没兴趣”，然后直接关上了门。我朋友觉得我太粗鲁，但我认为我是在节省大家的时间。行为 (Action): 在推销员面前关门 (Closing the door in a salesperson's face)类型 (Type): HISTORICAL (历史事件)得分 (Scores):


