TLDR: This study addresses the critical gap in evaluating LLM physical safety by developing a comprehensive benchmark for drone control and finds that larger models demonstrate better safety capabilities, particularly in refusing dangerous commands.},
	keywords = {Computer Science - Computers and Society, Computer Science - Artificial Intelligence, Computer Science - Machine Learning},
}

@article{ouyang_training_nodate,
	title = {Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback},
	abstract = {Making language models bigger does not inherently make them better at following a user’s intent. For example, large language models can generate outputs that are untruthful, toxic, or simply not helpful to the user. In other words, these models are not aligned with their users. In this paper, we show an avenue for aligning language models with user intent on a wide range of tasks by fine-tuning with human feedback. Starting with a set of labeler-written prompts and prompts submitted through a language model API, we collect a dataset of labeler demonstrations of the desired model behavior, which we use to fine-tune GPT-3 using supervised learning. We then collect a dataset of rankings of model outputs, which we use to further fine-tune this supervised model using reinforcement learning from human feedback. We call the resulting models InstructGPT. In human evaluations on our prompt distribution, outputs from the 1.3B parameter InstructGPT model are preferred to outputs from the 175B GPT-3, despite having 100x fewer parameters. Moreover, InstructGPT models show improvements in truthfulness and reductions in toxic output generation while having minimal performance regressions on public NLP datasets. Even though InstructGPT still makes simple mistakes, our results show that fine-tuning with human feedback is a promising direction for aligning language models with human intent.},
	language = {en},
	author = {Ouyang, Long and Wu, Jeff and Jiang, Xu and Almeida, Diogo and Wainwright, Carroll L and Mishkin, Pamela and Zhang, Chong and Agarwal, Sandhini and Slama, Katarina and Ray, Alex and Schulman, John and Hilton, Jacob and Kelton, Fraser and Miller, Luke and Simens, Maddie and Askell, Amanda and Welinder, Peter and Christiano, Paul and Leike, Jan and Lowe, Ryan},
	keywords = {reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF)},
	annote = {
好的，我来为你总结一下这篇论文 《Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback》（NeurIPS 2022，OpenAI 团队的工作）：
“Alignment is one of the fundamental toxicity mitigation approaches, which not only addresses overt expressions of toxicity but also reduces subtler biases” (Deng 等, 2024, p. 19)

📌 研究背景


大型语言模型（如 GPT-3）虽然功能强大，但常常出现 不真实、不安全或不符合用户意图 的输出。


传统的训练目标（预测下一个词）与用户真正需要的目标（帮助、诚实、无害）之间存在 错位。



🧩 方法：InstructGPT 的训练流程
研究提出了 基于人类反馈的强化学习 (RLHF) 来对 GPT-3 进行微调，形成 InstructGPT。主要步骤：


监督微调 (SFT)：收集人工编写的示范数据，用于训练模型。


奖励模型 (RM) 训练：人工对不同模型输出进行排序，训练一个奖励模型来预测人类偏好。


强化学习 (PPO)：使用奖励模型作为信号，进一步优化模型，使其更符合人类偏好。



📊 主要结果


更符合人类意图：在人工评估中，1.3B 参数的 InstructGPT 输出比 175B GPT-3 更受偏好。


提升真实性：在 TruthfulQA 数据集上，InstructGPT 的回答更真实，幻觉率（编造信息）显著降低。


减少有害输出：在 RealToxicityPrompts 数据集上，InstructGPT 生成的有害内容减少约 25\%。


泛化能力：即使在训练数据中很少见的任务（如代码总结、非英语指令），模型也能一定程度上遵循指令。


成本优势：相比直接训练更大的模型，使用 RLHF 微调的成本更低，但效果更好。



⚠️ 局限性与影响


局限性：


模型仍会犯简单错误（如接受错误前提、过度模棱两可）。


训练数据主要来自少数英语标注者，存在文化与价值观偏差。




社会影响：


提高了模型的可用性和安全性，但也可能被用于生成更具说服力的虚假信息。


强调了“模型到底对谁的价值观对齐”的重要性。





✨ 总结
这篇论文展示了 通过人类反馈微调（RLHF）可以显著改善语言模型的对齐性，使其更符合用户意图、更真实、更少有害。它提出的 InstructGPT 是后续 ChatGPT 等产品的重要基础。

要不要我帮你再画一个 流程图，把 SFT → RM → PPO 的训练过程直观地串联起来？这样你能更快理解 RLHF 的核心机制。
},
}

